course: biology
lesson: 242
title: Animal builders: beavers, dams and lodges
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A beaver is a builder with no tools at all: its teeth are the saw, its front paws are the hands, and its chest is the wheelbarrow for mud.
explain: When you see a tree in the park chewed into a pencil point, a beaver has been working there, cutting wood to build a dam across a stream.
explain: The dam holds back the water like the plug in a bath, so the stream becomes a deep pond that does not freeze to the bottom in winter.
explain: In the middle of the pond the family builds a lodge of sticks and mud with an underwater door, so wolves and bears cannot get in.
explain: The pond changes the whole valley: fish, frogs, insects and birds move in, so a beaver is called an ecosystem engineer.
fact: Beavers build dams of sticks, stones and mud to make a deep pond, and the biggest known beaver dam, in Canada, is about 850 metres long and shows up in satellite pictures.
fact: A beaver's front teeth keep growing all its life, and their orange front has iron in the enamel, which makes them harder; a beaver can cut down a thin tree in a few minutes.
fact: In experiments, beavers piled sticks on a loudspeaker playing the sound of running water, so the sound of a leak makes them build: much of their building is instinct, not planning.
fact: Beavers have no hammer or nails: they push sticks into the mud bottom, pile them up and plaster them with mud carried against their chests.
fact: A lodge has a dry room above the water, an underwater door, a store of branches kept underwater near by for winter food, a breathing hole at the top, and walls that freeze hard in winter so predators cannot dig in.
fact: A beaver family is the parents, this year's kits and last year's young, who help to build; young beavers leave at about two years old to make their own pond.
fact: A beaver slaps its flat scaly tail on the water to warn the family of danger; the tail also steers, stores fat and props the beaver up while it chews a tree.
fact: Beavers can stay underwater for about 15 minutes, and see-through eyelids work like swimming goggles.
fact: Some dams have been repaired for hundreds of years by family after family of beavers, each one fixing the dam it inherited.
fact: Beavers cannot control well which way a tree falls, and falling trees sometimes kill them; trees near water often lean towards the water anyway.
fact: Beavers were hunted to extinction in Britain about 400 years ago for their fur and are now being brought back to some rivers.
fact: Beaver ponds slow floods, clean water and keep streams flowing in dry seasons; in America green strips were seen along beaver streams during droughts and wildfires while the land around them burned.
fact: In the Ice Age there lived a giant beaver about as big as a black bear.
exercise: Why do beavers build a dam across a stream? | To make a deep pond that does not freeze to the bottom | To stop salmon and other fish from swimming upstream past them | To make a bridge so they can cross the stream | To dry out the stream so they can walk on it
exercise: What does a beaver use to cut down a tree? | Its strong front teeth | Its long sharp front claws | Its flat, heavy, scaly tail | A stone held in its paws
exercise: Why is the front of a beaver's teeth orange? | The enamel has iron in it, which makes it harder | Beavers eat a great many carrots, and carrots are orange | The teeth are rusty from all the water | The teeth are stained by mud
exercise: What happened when scientists played the sound of running water from a loudspeaker? | The beavers piled sticks on the loudspeaker | The beavers ran away from the noise and hid in their lodge | The beavers fell asleep beside it | The beavers did not notice it at all
exercise: What does the loudspeaker test show about beaver building? | Much of it is instinct, not careful planning | Beavers draw a plan in the mud with a stick before they build | Beavers only build when people are watching them | Beavers learn building from other animals, like otters
exercise: How does a beaver carry mud to its dam? | Held against its chest with its front paws | In a little bucket that it makes out of bark | In a pouch on its belly, like a kangaroo's | Balanced carefully on top of its flat tail
exercise: Where is the door of a beaver lodge? | Underwater | On the roof | Facing the forest | On the bank next to the dam
exercise: Why can a wolf not dig into a lodge in winter? | The mud walls freeze as hard as stone | The beavers guard the door all night | The lodge is built on a tall tree | Wolves are afraid of the water smell
exercise: Who lives in a beaver lodge? | The parents, this year's kits and last year's young | Only the mother beaver and her brand-new babies, without the father | One beaver alone, the others nearby | A crowd of many unrelated beavers
exercise: Why does a beaver slap its tail on the water? | To warn its family that danger is near | To catch a fish for its dinner with one quick smack | To make the cold water warmer for its kits | To scare away the biting mosquitoes in summer
exercise: About how long can a beaver stay underwater? | About 15 minutes | About 15 seconds | About 1 minute | About 3 hours
exercise: How do some beaver dams last for hundreds of years? | Family after family of beavers keeps repairing them | The beavers glue every stick in place with a special sticky spit | The mud turns into rock in a year | People repair them for the beavers
exercise: Why do beavers often fell trees standing near water? | Those trees tend to lean towards the water anyway | Trees near the water are always much softer to chew through | Beavers cannot walk on dry land for more than a step | Trees growing far from the water taste much too bitter
exercise: Why did beavers disappear from Britain about 400 years ago? | They were hunted for their fur | The rivers froze over for good | They all moved to Canada | Wolves ate every one of them
exercise: Why is a beaver called an ecosystem engineer? | Its pond changes the land and makes homes for many other animals | It builds strong wooden bridges for people to use on their walks | It cleans rubbish out of rivers | It plants new trees after cutting old ones
exercise: What did scientists see along beaver streams during droughts and wildfires? | Green strips of plants while the land around burned | The streams there dried up first and the plants burned quickest | Beavers putting out the fires | Nothing different from other land
exercise: What lived in the Ice Age? | A giant beaver about as big as a black bear | A beaver as big as an elephant, with tusks for teeth | A beaver that could fly between the treetops | A beaver that lived far out in the salty sea
test: In a cartoon a beaver builds a cosy house with a hammer, nails and a saw. How does a real beaver build? | With teeth, paws and mud, pushing sticks into the bottom and plastering them | With hammers, saws and other tools it steals from people at night | With sticks glued together with honey | It digs a hole and never builds anything
test: «there's a beaver dam on the news, my uncle reckons beavers plan it all like an architect» What is right? | No, experiments show much of it is instinct set off by the sound of running water | Yes, the oldest beaver in the family tells the others where each stick must go, one at a time | Yes, they draw plans in the mud first | No, people build the dams and beavers just live in them
test: «5 beavers on the dam. how many legs then» | 20 | 10 | 25 | 15
test: «3 beavers, no wait, 4 beavers slapping their tails. how many tails» | 4 | 3 | 8 | 12
test: What would happen if a farmer knocked a small hole in a beaver dam? | The beavers would hear the leak and soon fix it | The beavers would leave and never come back to that pond again | The pond would freeze straight away | Nothing, beavers never fix their dams
test: «a beaver chewed through a tree and it fell on him. thick animal» What is true? | Beavers cannot control well which way a tree falls, so it does sometimes happen | Beavers always make trees fall exactly where they want, every single time without fail | It cannot happen because beavers cut only dead trees | Beavers jump on top of the tree as it falls
test: Your friend says beavers being brought back to British rivers will only cause floods. What is closer to the truth? | Their ponds slow floods, clean water and make homes for wildlife | Beavers always flood the nearest towns and wash away the roads and farms | Beavers drink the rivers dry | Beavers have no effect on rivers at all
test: Why does a beaver pond keep a stream flowing in a dry summer? | The pond holds water and lets it out slowly | Beavers carry water from the sea | Beavers dig wells for the stream | The pond makes rain fall
test: «how do they even see underwater, mate, do they wear goggles lol» | They have see-through eyelids that work like goggles | They shut their eyes tight and swim blind, feeling their way | They see only with their whiskers | They keep their heads above the water
test: A young beaver is two years old. What will it most likely do soon? | Leave home to find a mate and build its own pond | Stay in the lodge with its parents for ever | Become the leader of its parents' family | Move into a fox's den
test: What does a beaver keep underwater near the lodge in autumn? | A pile of branches to eat in winter | A pile of stones that it uses for building | Its old teeth | Fish to eat in the spring, kept fresh in the cold
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the construction of a beaver dam? | It is constructed of sticks, stones and mud, without the employment of any tool | It is erected with implements appropriated from human dwellings | It is formed of ice deposited by the animals in winter | It is excavated, rather than built, in the bed of the stream
exam: Identify the principal function of the pond impounded behind a beaver dam. | The provision of deep water which does not freeze to the bed, wherein a protected lodge may be situated | The drowning of predators which approach the colony, chiefly wolves, bears and lynxes by night | The cultivation of fish for consumption by the beavers during the winter months, when they cannot leave the water | The irrigation of crops planted by the beavers
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning experiments in which the sound of flowing water was reproduced through a loudspeaker. | The beavers deposited sticks upon the loudspeaker, indicating that building is in large measure instinctive | The beavers dismantled the loudspeaker entirely and consumed its wooden and metal parts over a period of some days | The beavers abandoned the vicinity permanently | The beavers exhibited no response of any description
exam: Ascertain the reason for which the beaver is designated an ecosystem engineer. | Its works alter the landscape and furnish habitats for numerous other species | It is employed by engineers in the construction of bridges across wide rivers | It removes all vegetation from the banks of rivers | It confines its activity exclusively to its own lodge
